      "writtenOn": "2025-08-20T06:33:37Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "nit: wondering if we want to continue to pass the struct kvm_vcpu *vcpu as the PV interface input, or use vm_handle + vcpu_handle for the v6.15?\n\nLooks like this PV interface doesn\u0027t need to load pkvm_vcpu thus it is put at here, and passing vm_handle + vcpu_handle can directly using pkvm_get_vcpu() without exposing get_pkvm_vcpu_via_shared(). If this PV interface will use vm_handle + vcpu_handle, then probably all the other PV interfaces can do the same to avoid converting a host VA to pKVM VA, and accessing such pKVM VA to get the vm_handle + vcpu_handle.",
      "revId": "fef0c38714e0fdb9e53f55c06d9a23ec746b33b4",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "8fc6d0bd_d2cd864b",
        "filename": "arch/x86/kvm/pkvm/pkvm.c",
        "patchSetId": 13
      },
      "lineNbr": 1693,
      "author": {
        "id": 2022314
      },
      "writtenOn": "2025-08-20T08:52:41Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "What would be the advantage? Both vcpu_handle and vm_handle can be easily derived from the shared struct kvm_vcpu *vcpu, and that is exactly what get_pkvm_vcpu_via_shared() does, so why not use it?\n\nConverting host VA to pKVM VA is cheap (actually it doesn\u0027t cost at all), right? And get_pkvm_vcpu_via_shared() is still inlined for its users inside pkvm.c (I\u0027ve just checked that: in a kernel compiled simply with GCC without LTO, even though the `get_pkvm_vcpu_via_shared` symbol is exported, it is only referenced by pkvm_vm_mmu_map(), i.e. others still have it inlined).\n\n\u003e Looks like this PV interface doesn\u0027t need to load pkvm_vcpu thus it is put at here\n\nYes. BTW I was actually thinking about handling these MMU hypercalls in pkvm_vcpu_handle_kvm_call(), in the same unified way as all the other PV interfaces are handled there (even if that would mean unneeded loading/unloading of the vCPU), but then I realized that only this map hypercall needs to take a vCPU pointer, other MMU hypercalls implemented in next patches (unmap and age) don\u0027t need to do that and cannot really do that (since they are not always called in a context of a particular vCPU), so they just take the vm_handle instead.",
      "parentUuid": "c7b52d4f_ea4cf533",
      "revId": "fef0c38714e0fdb9e53f55c06d9a23ec746b33b4",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "7dafc1ea_00bc8b4d",
        "filename": "arch/x86/kvm/pkvm/pkvm.c",
        "patchSetId": 13
      },
      "lineNbr": 1693,
      "author": {
        "id": 1015280
      },
      "writtenOn": "2025-08-22T00:24:59Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Yes, it is cheap and not about the overhead. The benefit of using handle is to avoid the host using some malicious virtual address to break the pkvm hypervisor, e.g, cause page fault exceptions in the pkvm hypervisor as the pkvm hypervisor needs to access this malicious virtual address. Although DoS is not the target, preventing the host to break the pkvm hypervisor still seems attractive to me 😊 So raise this to have some early discussion to see your opinion.",
      "parentUuid": "8fc6d0bd_d2cd864b",
      "revId": "fef0c38714e0fdb9e53f55c06d9a23ec746b33b4",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
